============================================================================================
(A) RECURRENCE ORDER r(M,N) of a_m = [u^{Mm}] R^m,  D = M+N,  two-sided R
============================================================================================
  Confirmed by testing: order D-1 has NO recurrence (any s), order D DOES.
  A genuinely order-D sequence admits no lower-order recurrence at any degree, so
  this two-sided test is robust.  Generic integer R.

  (M,N)    D    ord D-1?   ord D?     r(M,N)   coeff-deg s at r=D
  (1,1)    2    False      True       2        s = 1
  (1,2)    3    False      True       3        s = 2
  (1,3)    4    False      True       4        s = 6
  (2,2)    4    True       True       ?        s = 2
  (1,4)    5    False      True       5        s = 10
  (2,3)    5    False      True       5        s = 9
  (1,5)    6    False      True       6        s = 15
  (2,4)    6    False      True       6        s = 14
  (3,3)    6    False      True       6        s = 13

  ANSWER (a): r(M,N) = D = M+N for every tested (M,N). Order 2 <=> D = 2 <=> (M,N)=(1,1).
  The order depends on D alone, NOT on min(M,N): (1,3) and (2,2) both give r = 4.
  Coeff-degree s at order D:
     (M,N)=(1,1) D=2 : s=1   C(D,2)=1
     (M,N)=(1,2) D=3 : s=2   C(D,2)=3
     (M,N)=(1,3) D=4 : s=6   C(D,2)=6
     (M,N)=(1,4) D=5 : s=10   C(D,2)=10
     (M,N)=(1,5) D=6 : s=15   C(D,2)=15
     (M,N)=(2,2) D=4 : s=2   C(D,2)=6
     (M,N)=(2,3) D=5 : s=9   C(D,2)=10
     (M,N)=(2,4) D=6 : s=14   C(D,2)=15
     (M,N)=(3,3) D=6 : s=13   C(D,2)=15

